@startuml
title RuleEngine - how a transfer is validated

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign direction
skinparam ParticipantPadding 12
skinparam BoxPadding 12

actor "Token holder" as Holder
participant "Token\n(CMTAT / ERC-3643)" as Token
participant "RuleEngine" as Engine
collections "Rule contracts\n(IRule)" as Rules

== State-changing path ==

alt standard transfer
    Holder -> Token : transfer(to, value)
    activate Token
    Token -> Token : spender = address(0)
    Token -> Engine : transferred(from, to, value)
else transferFrom, mint or burn
    Holder -> Token : transferFrom(from, to, value)\nmint(to, value) / burn(from, value)
    Token -> Token : spender = _msgSender()
    Token -> Engine : transferred(spender, from, to, value)
end

note over Token
    CMTAT picks the overload on spender != address(0)
    (ValidationModuleRuleEngine._callRuleEngineTransferred).
    A plain transfer has no spender, so it uses the
    3-argument overload.
end note

activate Engine

Engine -> Engine : onlyBoundToken

alt caller is not a bound token
    Engine -->> Token : revert\nRuleEngine_ERC3643Compliance_UnauthorizedCaller
    Token -->> Holder : transaction reverted
else caller is bound
    loop for each rule in _rules (capped by maxRules, default 10)
        Engine -> Rules : transferred(...)\nsame overload as the incoming call
        activate Rules
        alt rule forbids the transfer
            Rules -->> Engine : revert (no return value)
            Engine -->> Token : bubble up the revert
            Token -->> Holder : transaction reverted
            note right of Rules
                The whole transaction reverts on the
                first failing rule. Remaining rules
                are never reached.
            end note
        else rule allows the transfer
            Rules --> Engine : return
        end
        deactivate Rules
    end
    Engine --> Token : return
    Token --> Holder : transfer executed
end
deactivate Engine
deactivate Token

note over Token, Engine
    ERC-3643 tokens use the dedicated mint/burn entry points instead.
    Both take no spender and run the 3-argument rule loop:
    created(to, value)     -> _transferred(address(0), to, value)
    destroyed(from, value) -> _transferred(from, address(0), value)
end note

== Read-only path (never reverts) ==

Holder -> Engine : detectTransferRestriction(from, to, value)\ncanTransfer(from, to, value)
activate Engine
loop for each rule, until a rule returns a non-zero code
    Engine -> Rules : detectTransferRestriction(...)
    activate Rules
    Rules --> Engine : ERC-1404 restriction code
    deactivate Rules
end
Engine --> Holder : first non-zero code,\nor 0 when the transfer is allowed
deactivate Engine

@enduml
